-- title:
--   Descent closure: F_N = F_N^{full} from one-prime steps
-- statement:
--   Work inside the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, where for a nonzero natural number $d$ the ring homomorphism [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ d`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) substitutes $q \mapsto q^{d}$ (it reindexes exponents by multiplication by $d$), `jq` is the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant, and `jqN d` is its image `qExpand ℚ d jq`, i.e. $j(q^{d})$. For a nonzero natural number $N$, `modularFunctionField N` denotes the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(j(q), j(q^{N}))$ obtained by adjoining the two elements `jq` and `qExpand ℚ N jq` to $\mathbb{Q}$, while `modularFunctionFieldFull N` is the intermediate field obtained by adjoining the whole set of expansions $j(q^{d})$ for all nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$. The theorem fixes such an $N$ and assumes two families of hypotheses, indexed by the factorisations of $N$ as $N = M p$ with $M$ nonzero and $p$ prime: first, that $j(q^{M})$ lies in `modularFunctionField N`; second, that `modularFunctionField M = modularFunctionFieldFull M`. Its conclusion is that `modularFunctionField N = modularFunctionFieldFull N`, that is, $\mathbb{Q}(j(q), j(q^{N}))$ already contains $j(q^{d})$ for every divisor $d$ of $N$.
--
--   This is the assembling step of the generation theorem for the function field of $X_0(N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$: it propagates, along the divisor lattice, the statement that all expansions $j(q^{d})$ with $d \mid N$ are generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$. It is used in the induction that yields the generation statement at all levels and, with it, the degree computation $[\mathbb{Q}(j)(j_N):\mathbb{Q}(j)] = \psi(N)$ and the irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_N$.
